[FINDING] 2026-09-03 CRITICAL translations.js — 31 strings still carry the known "punctuation hard-coded at the front" RTL workaround bug (the exact `!هيا نبدأ` pattern the charter calls out). This is the old data-i18n system that drives story-prophet-adam.html (and the shared quiz/feedback keys reused by story-prophet-ibrahim.html). Examples, all in the `ar:` block:
  - translations.js:217 scene1TapPrompt: "!اضغط لتعرف كيف خُلق آدم" → should be "اضغط لتعرف كيف خُلق آدم!"
  - translations.js:231 scene3Result: "!سجدت جميع الملائكة لآدم" → "سجدت جميع الملائكة لآدم!"
  - translations.js:236 scene4Highlight: "!الكبر والغرور سيئان جداً! يجب أن نكون متواضعين دائماً" → "الكبر والغرور سيئان جداً! يجب أن نكون متواضعين دائماً!" (leading ! belongs at the very end, after "دائماً")
  - translations.js:245 scene5Activity: "!طابق الأسماء! اسحب كل عنصر إلى اسمه" → "طابق الأسماء! اسحب كل عنصر إلى اسمه!"
  - translations.js:287 scene10Counter: ":الأشخاص" → "الأشخاص:"
  - translations.js:301-303, 306-307, 326, 328-329, 332-333, 341, 345-352 — same pattern (quizTitle, resultsTitle, resultsScore, videoUnlock, moralText, feedbackCorrect, feedbackPerfect, feedbackSubhanAllah, feedbackThinkAgain, etc.)
  Full list of offending lines (grep `"[!:؟][^"]*"` in translations.js): 217, 230, 231, 236, 245, 253, 270, 281, 286, 287, 292, 296, 301, 302, 303, 306, 307, 326, 328, 329, 332, 333, 341, 345, 346, 347, 348, 349, 350, 351, 352.
  Fix is mechanical: move the leading `!`/`:` to the end of the string (or to the end of the correct clause where the string has two sentences, e.g. line 236, 270).
  Contrast: modalButton (translations.js:201) "هيا نبدأ!" is already correctly fixed — proves the pattern was known and partially patched but not swept through the whole file.

[FINDING] 2026-09-03 CRITICAL story-prophet-adam.html:390 — even where the HTML itself has a correctly-punctuated inline data-ar ("اكتملت القصة! التحدي الأخير!"), the element also carries data-i18n="quizTitle". bilingual.js applies data-ar first, then calls window.i18n.setLanguage(), which re-writes textContent from translations.js's buggy ar.quizTitle ("!اكتملت القصة! التحدي الأخير" — leading !). The correct inline value is clobbered by the old system on every language switch. Same double-attribute pattern likely affects any other data-i18n+data-ar element on this page — worth a dedicated pass to decide which system wins, or to delete the redundant data-ar since it is dead code today.

[FINDING] 2026-09-03 MAJOR story-prophet-adam.html:469-470 — nav arrows are hardcoded literal characters outside the translation system: `<span class="nav-arrow-left">←</span>` before "Back to Stories" and `<span class="nav-arrow-right">→</span>` after "Next Story". Neither span has data-ar/data-i18n, so in Arabic (RTL) mode the arrows keep pointing in their English/LTR sense — "Back" still points left, "Next" still points right — which is backwards once the flow direction is mirrored. Compare to story-prophet-nuh.html / story-prophet-ibrahim.html which embed the arrow inside the translated string itself (see next finding for the problem that creates).

[FINDING] 2026-09-03 MINOR story-prophet-nuh.html:249-250, story-prophet-ibrahim.html:403-404, story-prophet-yusuf.html:259-260 — nav-btn arrows are embedded as plain text in the English string ("← Back to Stories", "Next Story: Prophet Nuh →") but the arrow character is simply dropped from the corresponding data-ar value ("العودة إلى القصص", "القصة التالية: النبي نوح" — no arrow at all). Not wrong-direction, just missing in Arabic mode: the nav buttons lose their directional affordance entirely when the user switches to Arabic. Fix: add the (mirrored) arrow inside each data-ar string, e.g. data-ar="→ العودة إلى القصص" / "القصة التالية: النبي نوح ←" (RTL "back" points right, "next" points left).

